  "summary": "Reviews and a new detector proposal converge on quantum-enabled searches for dark matter, BSM physics, and gravitational waves.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of quantum-enabled or precision detection to test physics beyond the Standard Model. One review covers direct dark matter detection, including detector technologies, results since 2020, and future experiments. Proceedings discuss atomic clocks, atom interferometers, and magnetometers in space for BSM and dark matter searches. A white paper frames multi-messenger AGN observations and AGN-surrounding dark matter as BSM laboratories. A colloquium links isotope enrichment and detection to proposed quantum tests with radioactive atomic clocks. The one new detector concept proposes gravitational-wave sensing via massive-particle interferometry, where a gravitationally induced phase along a closed loop encodes the signal. The items are mixed in scope, but together they emphasize complementary laboratory, space-based, and astrophysical routes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
